# KDOS — the DDC/CI lines of a display controller, for ddcutil.
#
# THE SCOPING IS THE WHOLE RULE. /dev/i2c-* covers the graphics cards' DDC
# lines AND the chipset SMBus, and every DIMM's SPD EEPROM hangs off the SMBus:
# a stray write there is a machine that will not boot, not a lost setting. So
# the match walks up to the PCI function that owns the adapter and requires a
# display controller. Do not widen it to `KERNEL=="i2c-[0-9]*"` alone.
#
# 0x03* AND NOT 0x030000. PCI base class 0x03 is display controllers and
# nothing else — 0x0300 VGA-compatible, 0x0301 XGA, 0x0302 3D, 0x0380 other —
# and a discrete GPU with no legacy VGA decode reports 0x030200. Matching the
# full 0x030000 leaves those machines with no rule and ddcutil root-only, with
# no error to read. SMBus is base class 0x0c, subclass 0x05, so 0x03* cannot
# reach it.
#
# ONE ATTRS RUN, ONE DEVICE. Every ATTRS/KERNELS/DRIVERS/SUBSYSTEMS key in a
# contiguous run must be satisfied by the SAME ancestor: eudev tests the whole
# run against one device and restarts it a level up on any failure. So
# `ATTRS{name}=="*DDC*", ATTRS{class}=="0x03*"` matches nothing anywhere —
# `name` is on the i2c adapter and `class` is on the PCI function.
#
# Here GROUP= and MODE= DO work, unlike 70-kdos-backlight.rules: /dev/i2c-N is
# a real character device with a devnum.
#
# ddcutil ships its own 60-ddcutil-i2c.rules whose first rule is this one
# UNSCOPED, at GROUP="i2c"; its recipe deletes that file. Its second rule is
# `TAG+="uaccess"`, which grants nothing here — eudev has no uaccess builtin
# and there is no logind to consume the tag.

SUBSYSTEM=="i2c-dev", KERNEL=="i2c-[0-9]*", ATTRS{class}=="0x03*", GROUP="video", MODE="0660"
